WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.082 --> 00:00:00.915
(pensive music)

00:00:00.915 --> 00:00:01.748
- [Narrator] President Joe Biden

00:00:01.748 --> 00:00:04.080
and Republican House
Speaker, Kevin McCarthy,

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:07.380
have reached an agreement
to raise the debt ceiling.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.540
- We still have a lot of work to do,

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.490
but I believe this is an
agreement in principle

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.350
that's worthy of the American people.

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:15.300
- [Narrator] The agreement
still needs to be approved

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:18.660
by Congress before June 5th
when the government could run

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:20.700
short of money to pay its debts

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.100
and fund government programs.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.530
David Harrison covers the US economy

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:27.540
and the Federal Reserve for The Journal.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:29.220
He explains what's in the deal

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.710
and whether Democrats or Republicans,

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:33.540
if anyone, came out ahead.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:34.620
- The two big parts of this deal,

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:36.810
first of all, obviously, that
it raises the debt ceiling

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:38.610
for a couple years, basically pushes it

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:41.040
off to after the election.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:44.220
The Republicans really got
an agreement to curb spending

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:47.010
for next year and to cap
it for the subsequent year.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:50.385
- This deal is good news
I believe you'll see

00:00:50.385 --> 00:00:52.020
(coughs) for the American people.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:54.810
The agreement prevents
the worst possible crisis,

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:57.870
a default for the first time
in our nation's history.

00:00:57.870 --> 00:00:59.130
- There are a lot of other smaller things.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:00.960
There's new requirements that people

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.150
on food stamps either are employed

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.610
or are enrolled in some kind of program

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:08.130
and some provisions to streamline

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:12.000
or make it easier to get
energy projects approved.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.270
- President Biden has delivered a result

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:17.610
that avoids a catastrophic default

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:20.490
that prevents us from
our economy crashing.

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:23.910
- Democrats got spending cuts
that were not nearly as deep

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:27.450
as the Republicans had
initially put forward.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:30.000
Now comes the next phase,
which is Speaker McCarthy has

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.360
to sell this deal to his members,
to the House Republicans.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.310
- We did a conference
call with our conference,

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:38.160
and over 95% were overwhelmingly excited

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:39.120
about what they see.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.340
- A number of the more
conservative members

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.280
of the House GOP caucus have
come out in opposition to it,

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:47.910
calling it a disaster,
some calling it appalling,

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:49.440
basically saying that McCarthy gave

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.990
up too much to Democrats
to raise the debt ceiling.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.660
Likewise, on the Democratic
side, some of the sort

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:58.050
of more progressive
Democrats are very concerned

00:01:58.050 --> 00:01:59.670
about things like the work requirements

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:03.420
for aid programs, some
of the spending cuts.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:06.870
- Terrible policy,
absolutely terrible policy.

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:10.350
Does not reduce spending,
actually, by some estimates,

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:13.590
creates a burden on
administrative spending

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.840
that is actually worse, you know,

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.120
for the overall cost
of a program like that.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:21.150
- This bill's gonna have a
rough ride on Capitol Hill.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.220
We've already heard from
some folks on the right

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.050
and on the left who oppose it.

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.000
And so now the question is
whether there'll be enough

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.010
in the middle to carry it through.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.140
- We know, at any time
when you sit and negotiate

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:33.180
within two parties, that you gotta work

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:34.620
with both sides of the aisle,

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.290
so it's not 100% what everybody wants,

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.570
but when you look, the
country is gonna be stronger.

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:41.640
- [David] We have a June 5th deadline

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:42.750
to raise the debt ceiling

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:44.100
according to the Treasury Department.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.560
If Congress does not raise
the the debt ceiling by then,

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.000
our government could quite literally run

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.500
out of money to pay its bills.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:53.520
- I strongly urge both chambers
to pass that agreement.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:56.040
Let's keep moving forward
on meeting our obligations

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.980
and building the strongest economy

00:02:57.980 --> 00:02:59.265
in the history of the world.

00:02:59.265 --> 00:03:01.981
(pensive music continues)

